export const V6={title:'Clinical practice: examinations, restraint and feline handling (Skills V and VII)',hook:'A frightened 70-pound dog can injure a whole team; a stressed cat can scratch, bite or even become ill from fear. Good clinical practice starts with safe restraint and a calm approach. Skill V tests lifting and restraining a dog for a cephalic venipuncture, and Skill VII tests preparing a cat for a temperature using cat-friendly handling.',objectives:['Describe the history, physical examination and medical record in veterinary practice.','Lift dogs of different sizes and restrain a dog for cephalic venipuncture following HOSA Skill V.','Prepare a cat for a temperature using cat-friendly handling following HOSA Skill VII.','Describe routes of medication administration and common clinical procedures.'],sections:[
['History, exam and records',[
'A visit begins with the **signalment** (species, breed, age, sex and reproductive status), the **history** (reason for the visit, diet, medications, vaccination status, behavior and previous problems) and a **physical examination**: weight and body condition, temperature, pulse and respiration (**TPR**), mucous membranes and capillary refill, eyes, ears, mouth and teeth, lymph nodes, heart and lungs, abdomen, skin and coat, musculoskeletal and neurologic checks.',
'Medical records are legal documents. Many practices use the **SOAP** format—**Subjective** (history and observations), **Objective** (exam and test results), **Assessment** (problem list or diagnosis) and **Plan** (treatment, tests and client instructions). Entries are accurate, dated and signed; errors are corrected with a single line. Records also document vaccines, controlled drugs, consent forms and client communication.',
order('Order of a wellness visit',['Greet the client and patient','Take the signalment and history','Weigh the animal and assess body condition','Measure TPR and check mucous membranes','Perform the nose-to-tail physical exam','Record findings and the plan in the medical record'])]],
['Lifting dogs safely: Skill V steps 1–6',[
'Skill V has 15 steps in **4 minutes** (stuffed dogs are used). **1.** Hand hygiene. **2. Small dog onto the table:** hold the dog **draped over one forearm** with the other hand holding the head **just below the mandible**. **3.** Lift the small dog **off the table** to the floor using correct body mechanics. **4. Medium dog onto the table:** place **one arm around the neck** and the **other around the rear end** and lift. **5. Hold the medium dog standing:** one arm **around the neck** and the other **around the abdomen**, **hugging the dog close** to your body.',
'**6.** **Verbalize lifting a large dog:** large dogs (over about 40–50 pounds) are lifted by **two people**—one supporting the **chest and neck** and the other the **abdomen and hindquarters**—lifting together on a count, bending at the knees and keeping the back straight. A lift table or ramp may be used. Never lift by the legs or scruff a dog.',
order('Lift a medium-sized dog',['Approach calmly and speak to the dog','Place one arm around the neck','Place the other arm around the rear end','Lift with your legs, keeping your back straight','Set the dog on the table','Hold standing: arm around the neck, other around the abdomen, hug close'])]],
['Restraint for cephalic venipuncture: Skill V steps 7–15',[
'The judge says, “Place the dog in a sitting position for a cephalic venipuncture.” **7.** Place the dog **on the table near one end, facing the edge, sitting**. **8.** Restrain the head by **reaching under the neck**, placing the hand **behind the jaw** and **pressing the head against your chest**. **9.** Use the other arm to reach around the **rear of the dog** and pull it close to your body. **10.** Move the arm that held the rear **across the shoulders** and grasp the **leg opposite your body**.',
'**11.** Hold the leg by placing the dog’s **elbow in the palm** of that hand and **pushing the leg forward**. **12.** Place your **thumb on top of the leg**, **squeezing and rotating laterally** to **occlude (raise) the cephalic vein**. When the judge says the venipuncture is complete, **13.** **release your thumb** (so the vein is no longer occluded and pressure can be applied). **14.** Communicate calmly. **15.** Hand hygiene.',
order('Cephalic restraint',['Dog sitting near the table end, facing the edge','Arm under the neck; hand behind the jaw; head against your chest','Other arm around the rear; pull the dog close','Move that arm across the shoulders; grasp the far leg','Elbow in your palm; push the leg forward','Thumb on top; squeeze and rotate laterally to raise the vein','Release your thumb when blood is drawn'])]],
['Cat-friendly handling: Skill VII',[
'Skill VII has 17 steps in **6 minutes** (real feline visits should take at least 15 minutes, following the AAFP motto “**Go Slow to Go Fast**”). **1.** Hand hygiene. **2.** Gather and prepare equipment. **3.** Spray the exam area with a **synthetic feline facial pheromone (FFP) analog** to promote calm. **4.** Greet the cat and client **by name** with **moderated body language and voice**. **5.** Place the carrier on the exam table and **open the carrier door as soon as you enter**. **6.** Verbalize that the history has been obtained. **7.** If the cat came out, **place the carrier out of sight** until procedures are done.',
'If the cat stays in the carrier: **8.** place a **towel between the two halves** of the carrier as you **remove the top and front**, and **9.** create a towel “**tent**” over the cat so it stays accessible. **10.** **Lightly swaddle** the cat in a towel (or let it stay in the carrier bottom). **11.** Place your hand on top of the cat’s head with fingers toward the nose, using the **first and fifth fingers** to hold each side of the head. **12.** **Massage or stroke** the top of the head to relax the cat. **13.** **Watch for signs of anxiety**—posture, ears, pupils, tail—and report them. When the judge says the temperature has been taken, **14.** release the cat, **15.** verbalize that the owner will comfort the cat and return it to the carrier, **16.** stay calm and consistent and **17.** perform hand hygiene.',
order('Feline temperature prep',['Pheromone spray in the exam area','Greet the cat and client by name calmly','Open the carrier door as soon as you enter','Carrier out of sight if the cat came out—or remove the top with a towel tent if not','Lightly swaddle in a towel','Hand on top of the head; first and fifth fingers on each side','Stroke the head; watch for anxiety','Release; owner comforts the cat'])]],
['Medications and procedures',[
'Routes of administration include **oral (PO)**, **subcutaneous (SQ)**—under the loose skin, often between the shoulder blades in dogs and cats—**intramuscular (IM)**, **intravenous (IV)**, **topical**, **ophthalmic**, **otic** and **intranasal**. Labels list the drug, strength, dose, route, frequency, patient and client names and prescriber. Withdrawal times apply to food animals. Clients learn how to pill pets, give eye and ear medications and complete courses of antibiotics.',
'Common procedures include **nail trims**, **ear cleaning**, **anal gland expression**, **microchipping**, **vaccinations**, **radiographs** and **blood collection** (cephalic, saphenous or jugular veins in dogs and cats; jugular in horses; tail (coccygeal) or jugular vein in cattle). Restraint must match the species: towels and cat bags for cats, halters and stocks for horses and cattle, and squeeze chutes for cattle.',
match('Species and blood draw site',[['Dog','Cephalic, saphenous or jugular vein'],['Cat','Cephalic, medial saphenous or jugular vein'],['Horse','Jugular vein'],['Cattle','Tail (coccygeal) or jugular vein'],['SQ injection site in dogs','Loose skin between the shoulder blades'],['Stocks or chute','Restraint for large animals']])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Handling choices',{a:{text:'A cat will not come out of its carrier and is crouched with dilated pupils. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you do?',choices:[['Remove the top with a towel between the halves and create a towel tent','b','Correct. The cat stays covered and accessible.',true],['Dump the cat out of the carrier','b','Forcing a cat out increases fear and risk of injury.',false]]},b:{text:'During the temperature, the cat’s ears flatten and its tail lashes. What next?',choices:[['Report the signs, slow down and keep handling gentle','c','Correct. Observe and report anxiety.',true],['Restrain more tightly','c','More force increases stress.',false]]},c:{text:'The judge says the temperature has been taken. You release the cat and the owner returns it to the carrier.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Visits include signalment, history, nose-to-tail exam and TPR; records use SOAP, are legal documents and are corrected with a single line.','Skill V: small dog over the forearm with the head held below the mandible; medium dog with arms around the neck and rear; large dog lifted by two people; cephalic restraint with head against your chest, elbow in palm, leg forward, thumb rotating laterally to raise the vein, then release.','Skill VII: pheromone spray, calm greeting, open the carrier immediately, towel tent or swaddle, first and fifth fingers on the head, stroke, watch for anxiety and “go slow to go fast.”'],terms:[['Signalment','Species, breed, age, sex and reproductive status of a patient.'],['Cephalic vein','A vein on the front of the foreleg used for blood draws.'],['Feline facial pheromone','A calming scent analog sprayed in exam rooms.']],mcqs:[q('How do you hold a small dog when lifting it?','Draped over one forearm with the other hand under the mandible','By the front legs','By the scruff','By the tail','Per Skill V.'),q('For cephalic restraint, the dog is placed:','Sitting near the table end, facing the edge','Lying on its back','Standing in the middle','On the floor','Step 7.'),q('How is the cephalic vein occluded?','Thumb on top of the leg, squeezing and rotating laterally','Pressing the neck','Pulling the tail','Holding the paw only','Raises the vein.'),q('When is the thumb released?','After the blood is drawn','Before the needle enters','Never','At the start','Then pressure is applied.'),q('How many people should lift a large dog?','Two','One','Four','None','Team lift.'),q('What is sprayed in the exam area in Skill VII?','A synthetic feline facial pheromone analog','Disinfectant on the cat','Perfume','Water','Promotes calm.'),q('When should the carrier door be opened?','As soon as you enter the room','After the exam','Never','Only if the cat meows','Lets the cat come out on its own.'),q('The AAFP motto for feline visits is:','Go slow to go fast','Fast is best','Restrain early','No touching','Calmer cats are easier to examine.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how to lift and restrain a medium-sized dog for a cephalic venipuncture following HOSA Skill V, and how to prepare an anxious cat for a temperature following Skill VII.',rubric:[['Lifting',['arm around the neck','rear end','body mechanics','hug','abdomen']],['Cephalic restraint',['sitting','facing the edge','behind the jaw','chest','across the shoulders','elbow','palm','thumb','rotate laterally','release']],['Feline setup',['pheromone','greet','name','open the carrier','out of sight','towel','tent']],['Feline handling',['swaddle','first and fifth fingers','stroke','anxiety','report','calm','go slow']]],model:'After hand hygiene, I lift a medium dog onto the table with one arm around its neck and the other around its rear end, lifting with my legs and keeping my back straight, then hold it standing with one arm around the neck and the other around the abdomen, hugging it close. For a cephalic venipuncture, I place the dog sitting near one end of the table, facing the edge. I reach under the neck, put my hand behind the jaw and press the head against my chest, use my other arm around the rear to pull the dog close, then move that arm across the shoulders and grasp the leg farthest from my body. I cup the dog’s elbow in my palm and push the leg forward, place my thumb on top of the leg and squeeze while rotating laterally to raise the cephalic vein, and release my thumb once the blood is drawn. For an anxious cat, I spray the exam area with a feline facial pheromone analog, greet the cat and client by name with a calm voice and body language, set the carrier on the table and open the door right away. If the cat comes out, I put the carrier out of sight; if not, I place a towel between the halves while removing the top and front and make a towel tent so the cat stays covered. I lightly swaddle the cat, rest my hand on top of its head with my first and fifth fingers on each side, stroke its head, watch for signs of anxiety such as flattened ears or dilated pupils and report them, staying calm throughout—“go slow to go fast.”'},sources:[RULES,AAFP,FEARFREE,MERCK]};

export const V7={title:'Laboratory procedures and fecal flotation (Skill IV)',hook:'Many parasites live quietly inside an animal until they cause anemia, diarrhea or weight loss. A simple fecal flotation—floating parasite eggs to the top of a special solution—can find them in minutes. The veterinary lab also runs blood counts, urinalyses and skin tests that turn symptoms into diagnoses.',objectives:['Use a microscope and follow laboratory safety and quality control.','Perform a simple fecal flotation following HOSA Skill IV.','Recognize common parasite eggs and other fecal tests.','Describe blood tests, urinalysis and skin and ear diagnostics.'],sections:[
['The veterinary laboratory and microscope',[
'In-clinic labs run **fecal exams**, **blood tests** (CBC, chemistry panels, heartworm and FeLV/FIV snap tests), **urinalysis**, **skin scrapings**, **ear cytology** and **fungal cultures**. Results guide diagnosis and treatment the same day. Lab safety includes gloves, hand hygiene, no eating or drinking, proper disposal of biological waste and sharps, and labeling every sample with the patient’s name, date and sample type.',
'The **compound (binocular) microscope** magnifies with an **ocular lens** (10×) and **objective lenses** (commonly 4×, 10×, 40× and 100× oil immersion); total magnification is ocular × objective. Start with the **lowest power**, focus with the **coarse focus**, then the **fine focus**, and use **immersion oil** only with the 100× lens. Carry the microscope with two hands—one on the **arm** and one under the **base**.',
"Clean the lenses only with lens paper, lower the stage and return to the lowest objective before storing the microscope, and cover it to keep out dust. A sharp, well-lit image depends on adjusting the **condenser** and **diaphragm** for the specimen being viewed.",
match('Microscope parts',[['Ocular lens','Eyepiece, usually 10×'],['Objective lens','Lens near the slide, 4× to 100×'],['Stage','Platform that holds the slide'],['Coarse focus','Large adjustment, used first'],['Fine focus','Small adjustment for sharp detail'],['Immersion oil','Used only with the 100× objective']])]],
['Simple fecal flotation: Skill IV',[
'A **fecal flotation** uses a solution denser than parasite eggs (such as sodium nitrate, zinc sulfate or Sheather’s sugar solution) so eggs **float** to the top. Skill IV has 12 steps in **5 minutes**. **1.** Hand hygiene. **2.** Put on **non-latex exam gloves**. **3.** Assemble equipment and supplies. **4.** Place about **2–5 g of feces** in a paper cup. **5.** Add **30 mL of flotation solution**. **6.** With a **tongue depressor**, mix to an **evenly suspended emulsion**.',
'**7.** Pour the suspension through a **10 cm (4 × 4 inch) gauze square** into another cup to strain out debris. **8.** Fill a **shell vial** (or 15 mL conical centrifuge tube) with the filtered solution to form a **convex dome (meniscus)** at the rim. **9.** Place a **cover slip** on top of the meniscus. **10.** Verbalize leaving the cover slip in place for **10–20 minutes**. **11.** Lift the cover slip **straight up** and place it on a **glass slide, fluid side down**. **12.** Remove gloves and perform hand hygiene. The slide is then scanned at 10× for eggs and cysts.',
order('Fecal flotation steps',['Hand hygiene and gloves','2–5 g of feces in a paper cup','Add 30 mL of flotation solution','Mix to an even emulsion with a tongue depressor','Strain through a 4 × 4 gauze into another cup','Fill the vial to a convex meniscus','Cover slip on top; wait 10–20 minutes','Lift straight up; place fluid side down on a slide'])]],
['Parasite eggs and other fecal tests',[
'Common findings in dogs and cats include **roundworm** (*Toxocara*) eggs (round with a thick, pitted shell), **hookworm** eggs (oval, thin-shelled with a morula of cells), **whipworm** eggs (lemon or football-shaped with plugs at both ends), **tapeworm** segments or egg packets, **coccidia** oocysts and **Giardia** cysts. **Centrifugal flotation** detects more eggs than simple flotation; **direct smears** show motile protozoa; **sedimentation** finds heavy eggs such as flukes; antigen tests detect Giardia.',
'Fresh samples give the best results; refrigerate if testing will be delayed. Report findings to the veterinarian, who prescribes the correct dewormer—different parasites need different drugs—and advises on environmental cleanup and zoonotic risk.',
match('Egg identification',[['Thick, pitted, round shell','Roundworm'],['Oval, thin shell with a cell cluster','Hookworm'],['Lemon-shaped with plugs at both ends','Whipworm'],['Rice-like segments near the anus','Tapeworm'],['Small oocysts in puppies with diarrhea','Coccidia'],['Cysts best found with antigen tests','Giardia']])]],
['Blood tests',[
'A **complete blood count (CBC)** measures red blood cells (including **packed cell volume**, PCV), white blood cells and a **differential**, and platelets. A **PCV** is measured by spinning blood in a microhematocrit tube in a centrifuge; the **buffy coat** above the red cells contains white cells and platelets, and **total protein** is read from the plasma with a **refractometer**. A **blood smear** shows cell shapes and counts. Manual counting systems such as the **Leukopet** dilute blood for white cell and platelet counts.',
'**Chemistry panels** assess organs: BUN and creatinine (kidneys), ALT and ALP (liver), glucose, electrolytes and proteins. **SNAP tests** quickly detect heartworm antigen, FeLV antigen, FIV antibodies, parvovirus and tick-borne diseases. Blood is collected into the correct **Vacutainer** tubes—lavender (EDTA) for CBCs, red or tiger-top tubes for serum chemistry—and labeled immediately.',
sort('Which test?',['CBC or PCV','Chemistry panel','SNAP test'],[['Anemia check',0],['Kidney values BUN and creatinine',1],['Heartworm antigen',2],['White blood cell count',0],['Liver enzymes',1],['FeLV and FIV status',2]])]],
['Urinalysis, skin and ear tests',[
'**Urine** may be collected by free catch, catheterization or **cystocentesis** (a needle through the abdominal wall into the bladder, giving a sterile sample). A urinalysis includes physical tests (color, clarity, **specific gravity** by refractometer), chemistry by **reagent strip** (pH, protein, glucose, ketones, blood, bilirubin) and **sediment** examination for cells, casts, crystals and bacteria.',
'**Skin scrapings** look for mites (deep scrapes for Demodex; superficial for Sarcoptes). **Tape preps** and **impression smears** find yeast and bacteria; **ear cytology** identifies yeast, bacteria and ear mites. **Dermatophyte (ringworm) cultures** and the **Wood’s lamp** help detect fungal infection. **Colostrometers** check colostrum quality on farms.',
"Quality control keeps results trustworthy: run controls on analyzers as directed, check expiration dates on reagents and test kits, calibrate refractometers with distilled water (specific gravity 1.000), label samples immediately and record results in the medical record. A result that does not fit the animal’s condition should be repeated before the veterinarian acts on it.",
match('Test and purpose',[['Cystocentesis','Sterile urine sample from the bladder'],['Refractometer','Measures urine specific gravity and plasma protein'],['Reagent strip','Urine chemistry dipstick'],['Deep skin scraping','Finds Demodex mites'],['Ear cytology','Detects yeast, bacteria and mites'],['Wood’s lamp','Screens for some ringworm']])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Fecal results',{a:{text:'Your flotation slide shows lemon-shaped eggs with plugs at both ends. (Scenario invented for practice.) What are they?',choices:[['Whipworm eggs','b','Correct. Bipolar plugs are characteristic.',true],['Roundworm eggs','b','Roundworm eggs are round and pitted.',false]]},b:{text:'What should you do with the result?',choices:[['Record it and report it to the veterinarian for treatment','c','Correct. The veterinarian chooses the dewormer.',true],['Throw away the slide and say nothing','c','Results must be documented and reported.',false]]},c:{text:'The veterinarian prescribes an appropriate dewormer and advises yard cleanup. Lab work led to the right treatment.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Use the microscope from low power with coarse then fine focus; oil only with 100×; carry by the arm and base; label every sample.','Skill IV: gloves, 2–5 g feces, 30 mL solution, mix, strain through 4 × 4 gauze, fill the vial to a convex meniscus, cover slip 10–20 minutes, lift straight up onto a slide fluid side down.','Know roundworm, hookworm, whipworm, tapeworm, coccidia and Giardia; CBC/PCV, chemistry and SNAP tests; urinalysis by refractometer, reagent strip and sediment; skin scrapings and ear cytology.'],terms:[['Fecal flotation','A test that floats parasite eggs to the surface of a dense solution.'],['Packed cell volume','The percentage of blood made up of red blood cells.'],['Cystocentesis','Collecting urine with a needle directly from the bladder.']],mcqs:[q('How much feces is used in Skill IV?','About 2–5 g','About 50 g','A single drop','100 g','Per the rating sheet.'),q('How much flotation solution is added?','30 mL','5 mL','100 mL','1 L','Per the rating sheet.'),q('How long does the cover slip stay on the meniscus?','10–20 minutes','1 minute','1 hour','24 hours','Eggs float up.'),q('How is the cover slip placed on the slide?','Fluid side down','Fluid side up','Sideways','Folded','Lifted straight up first.'),q('Lemon-shaped eggs with plugs at both ends are:','Whipworm','Roundworm','Hookworm','Tapeworm','Bipolar plugs.'),q('Which microscope lens uses immersion oil?','100× objective','4× objective','10× objective','The ocular','Only the oil lens.'),q('A sterile urine sample is collected by:','Cystocentesis','Free catch','From the litter box','From the floor','Needle into the bladder.'),q('Which test detects heartworm antigen quickly?','SNAP test','Urinalysis','Skin scraping','Ear cytology','In-clinic test.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how to perform a simple fecal flotation following HOSA Skill IV and explain how the results guide treatment and protect people.',rubric:[['Preparation',['hand hygiene','gloves','assemble','2-5 g','paper cup','30 ml','solution']],['Technique',['tongue depressor','emulsion','gauze','strain','vial','meniscus','cover slip','10-20 minutes']],['Slide and reading',['straight up','glass slide','fluid side down','microscope','10x','eggs']],['Interpretation',['roundworm','hookworm','whipworm','dewormer','veterinarian','zoonotic','record']]],model:'I wash my hands, put on non-latex gloves and assemble the flotation solution, paper cups, tongue depressor, gauze, shell vial or conical tube, cover slip and glass slide. I place about 2–5 grams of feces in a paper cup, add 30 mL of flotation solution and mix with the tongue depressor until the feces form an evenly suspended emulsion. I pour the mixture through a 4 × 4 inch gauze square into a second cup to strain out debris, then fill the shell vial with the filtered solution until it forms a convex dome, or meniscus, at the rim. I place a cover slip on the meniscus and leave it for 10–20 minutes so the lighter parasite eggs float up and stick to it. I lift the cover slip straight up, place it fluid side down on a glass slide, remove my gloves and wash my hands. The slide is scanned under the microscope at 10× for eggs and oocysts. Finding roundworm, hookworm, whipworm or other eggs tells the veterinarian which dewormer to prescribe and when to recheck. Because roundworms and hookworms can infect people, the results also guide client education about picking up feces promptly, hand washing and keeping children from contaminated soil.'},sources:[RULES,CAPC,MERCK]};

export const V8={title:'Emergency care and first aid',hook:'A dog hit by a car, a cat that ate a lily, a Labrador collapsing on a hot day, a puppy choking on a toy—emergencies happen fast, and what happens in the first minutes matters most. Emergency care and first aid is 12% of the test, and much of it comes from the American Red Cross Pet First Aid app.',objectives:['Perform a primary assessment and recognize life-threatening signs in animals.','Provide first aid for bleeding, shock, fractures, burns and choking.','Respond to heatstroke, hypothermia, poisoning, allergic reactions, seizures and GDV.','Describe CPR and safe transport of injured animals.'],sections:[
['Approaching an injured animal',[
'First, make sure the scene is **safe** for you. Injured animals in pain may bite even their owners. Approach slowly and calmly, and use a **muzzle** (commercial or made from gauze, a leash or a strip of fabric) on dogs that are not vomiting or having trouble breathing; cover cats with a towel. Call the veterinarian or emergency clinic early so they can prepare.',
'Check the **ABCs**: **airway** (open and clear), **breathing** (rate and effort) and **circulation** (pulse, gum color and capillary refill). **Emergency signs** include difficulty breathing, pale, blue or bright red gums, collapse, uncontrolled bleeding, seizures, distended abdomen, inability to urinate (especially male cats), severe vomiting or diarrhea, suspected poisoning and major trauma.',
order('Primary approach',['Make sure the scene is safe','Approach calmly; muzzle or towel as appropriate','Check the airway','Check breathing','Check pulse, gum color and capillary refill','Control bleeding; call the veterinarian'])]],
['Bleeding, shock, fractures and wounds',[
'Control **bleeding** with **direct pressure** using a clean cloth or gauze; add layers instead of removing soaked ones, and elevate a limb if possible. Tourniquets are a last resort. **Shock**—from blood loss, trauma, heatstroke or severe illness—causes **pale gums**, slow capillary refill, a rapid weak pulse, cool legs and weakness. Keep the animal **warm and quiet**, keep the head level and transport promptly.',
'**Fractures** should be immobilized if it can be done safely (padded splint including the joints above and below) or the animal kept still on a firm surface; do not try to set a bone. **Burns** are cooled with cool (not ice-cold) water and covered loosely. **Wounds** are covered to prevent contamination. Bite wounds can look small but hide deep damage and infection.',
"**Eye injuries**, **lameness** with a dangling limb, **difficult birth** (straining more than about 30–60 minutes without producing a puppy or kitten) and **snake bites** are also emergencies; keep the animal calm and still and go to the veterinarian.",
sort('Correct first aid?',['Correct','Incorrect'],[['Direct pressure on a bleeding wound',0],['Removing soaked gauze to check the wound',1],['Keeping a shocked animal warm',0],['Trying to straighten a broken leg',1],['Cooling a burn with cool water',0],['Putting butter on a burn',1]])]],
['Heat, cold and breathing emergencies',[
'**Heatstroke** is common in hot weather, in cars and in brachycephalic breeds: signs include heavy panting, drooling, bright red gums, vomiting, collapse and a temperature above about **104 °F**. Move the animal to a cool area, cool it with **cool (not ice-cold) water**, use fans and get to the veterinarian; stop cooling at about **103 °F** to avoid overcooling. Never leave pets in parked cars. **Hypothermia** and **frostbite** require gradual warming with blankets and veterinary care.',
'**Choking** causes pawing at the mouth, gagging and difficulty breathing. Look in the mouth and remove a visible object carefully (avoid being bitten or pushing it deeper); if the animal cannot breathe, perform modified **Heimlich** thrusts (abdominal thrusts or back blows for small animals) as taught in pet first aid. **Allergic reactions**—facial swelling, hives, bumps on the face and muzzle, itching and licking—may follow insect stings or medications; severe reactions with breathing difficulty are emergencies.',
match('Emergency and sign',[['Heatstroke','Heavy panting, bright red gums, collapse'],['Hypothermia','Shivering, low temperature, lethargy'],['Choking','Pawing at the mouth and gagging'],['Allergic reaction','Facial swelling, hives and itching'],['Shock','Pale gums and rapid weak pulse'],['GDV','Swollen abdomen and unproductive retching']])]],
['Poisoning, seizures and GDV',[
'For **poisoning**, identify the substance, amount and time; keep the package; and call the veterinarian or an animal poison control center **before** doing anything. **Do not induce vomiting** unless told to—it is dangerous with corrosives, petroleum products and in animals that are drowsy or seizing. Common poisons include chocolate, xylitol, grapes, lilies, rodenticides, antifreeze, human medications and insecticides.',
'During a **seizure**, keep the animal away from stairs and objects, do not put hands near the mouth, time the seizure and keep the area quiet and dim; seizures lasting longer than about 5 minutes or occurring in clusters are emergencies. **Gastric dilatation-volvulus (GDV)** in large deep-chested dogs—swollen abdomen, restlessness and unproductive retching—requires immediate veterinary surgery.',
"In horses, **colic** (abdominal pain shown by pawing, rolling, looking at the flank and not eating) is the most common emergency; call the veterinarian, remove feed and keep the horse from injuring itself. In cattle, **bloat** (left-side abdominal swelling) and **difficult calving** need prompt help.",
chk('A dog just ate a box of raisins. What should the owner do first?',['Call the veterinarian or animal poison control right away','Wait to see if symptoms appear','Give milk','Induce vomiting with salt'],'Grapes and raisins can cause kidney failure; get advice immediately.')]],
['CPR and transport',[
'If an animal is unresponsive and not breathing, begin **CPR** (veterinary guidelines from the RECOVER Initiative): place the animal on its side (deep-chested dogs) or back (barrel-chested dogs such as Bulldogs), compress the chest **100–120 times per minute** at about **one-third to one-half** of the chest width, allowing full recoil, and give **rescue breaths** by closing the mouth and breathing into the nose, at a ratio of **30 compressions to 2 breaths** when alone. For cats and small dogs, compress over the heart with one hand around the chest. Continue while transporting to a veterinary hospital.',
'Transport injured animals on a **flat, firm surface** (board, blanket held taut or a box for small animals) to protect the spine, keeping them warm and restrained. Drive carefully and call ahead. A **pet first aid kit** includes gauze, bandage rolls, tape, a muzzle, a digital thermometer, saline, a blanket, gloves, the veterinarian’s phone number and the poison control number.',
order('Pet CPR basics',['Check responsiveness and breathing','Position the animal for compressions','Compress 100–120 times per minute','Allow full chest recoil','Give 2 breaths into the nose after 30 compressions','Continue while transporting to the veterinarian'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Hot-day collapse',{a:{text:'A Bulldog collapses after a walk on a hot afternoon. It is panting heavily with bright red gums. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you suspect?',choices:[['Heatstroke','b','Correct. Brachycephalic breeds overheat easily.',true],['Allergic reaction','b','The setting and signs point to heatstroke.',false]]},b:{text:'What first aid is best?',choices:[['Move to shade, cool with cool water and fans and go to the veterinarian','c','Correct. Avoid ice-cold water.',true],['Wrap the dog in a warm blanket','c','That would worsen overheating.',false]]},c:{text:'You stop cooling around 103 °F and transport the dog for treatment of complications.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Ensure safety, muzzle (unless vomiting or breathing difficulty) or towel, check ABCs and call ahead; know emergency signs such as pale or blue gums, collapse and straining male cats.','Direct pressure for bleeding, keep shocked animals warm, immobilize fractures without setting them, cool burns and heatstroke with cool—not ice-cold—water; facial bumps and licking suggest an allergic reaction.','Call poison control before inducing vomiting; protect seizing animals and time seizures; GDV needs surgery; CPR at 100–120/min with 30:2 breaths; transport on a firm, flat surface.'],terms:[['Heatstroke','Dangerous overheating with temperature above about 104 °F.'],['Shock','Poor blood flow to tissues causing pale gums and weakness.'],['Muzzle','A device that prevents biting during handling.']],mcqs:[q('A dog exhibiting bumps on its face and muzzle and licking its flank is most likely experiencing:','An allergic reaction','Bleeding','A torsion','Frostbite','HOSA sample question.'),q('When should a muzzle NOT be used?','When the animal is vomiting or has trouble breathing','When the animal is in pain','When the animal is frightened','During transport','Airway first.'),q('How should heatstroke be treated?','Cool water and fans, then the veterinarian','Ice baths','Warm blankets','Nothing','Avoid overcooling.'),q('What should be done first for suspected poisoning?','Call the veterinarian or poison control','Induce vomiting','Give milk','Wait and watch','Get expert advice.'),q('Pale gums with a rapid weak pulse suggest:','Shock','Heatstroke','Health','Allergy only','Keep warm and quiet.'),q('Compression rate for pet CPR is about:','100–120 per minute','30 per minute','200 per minute','60 per minute','RECOVER guidelines.'),q('Swollen abdomen and unproductive retching in a Great Dane suggest:','GDV','Heatstroke','Allergic reaction','Hypothermia','Bloat emergency.'),q('How should an injured dog with a possible back injury be moved?','On a flat, firm surface','By the legs','In someone’s arms bent over','By the scruff','Protect the spine.')],frq:{prompt:'A dog has been hit by a car. It is conscious but bleeding from a back leg, has pale gums and may have a broken leg. Describe the first aid steps from approaching the dog to arriving at the veterinary hospital.',rubric:[['Safety and approach',['safe','calm','muzzle','vomiting','breathing','towel']],['ABCs and bleeding',['airway','breathing','circulation','direct pressure','layers','elevate']],['Shock and fracture',['shock','pale','warm','quiet','immobilize','splint','do not set']],['Transport',['flat','firm','board','blanket','call ahead','veterinarian']]],model:'First I make sure the road is safe for me and other people, then approach slowly and calmly, speaking softly, because an injured dog in pain may bite. If the dog is not vomiting or struggling to breathe, I place a muzzle made from a leash or gauze. I check the ABCs: the airway is clear, breathing is present and the pale gums and fast, weak pulse suggest shock. I control the bleeding from the back leg with firm, direct pressure using clean gauze or cloth, adding more layers if blood soaks through instead of removing the first ones. To manage shock, I keep the dog warm with a blanket and as quiet as possible. I do not try to straighten the leg; if it can be done safely, I support it with a padded splint that includes the joints above and below, or I keep the dog still. I move the dog onto a flat, firm surface such as a board or a blanket held taut by two people to protect the spine, keep it covered and restrained, call the veterinary hospital so the team can prepare and drive carefully, continuing pressure on the wound until we arrive.'},sources:[RULES,REDCROSS_PET,ASPCA_POISON,RECOVER,MERCK]};

export const V9={title:'Principles of surgery: asepsis, anesthesia, operative site preparation and surgical packs (Skills III and VI)',hook:'Every spay, neuter and fracture repair depends on a sterile field, safe anesthesia and careful patient preparation. Most surgical infections come from the patient’s own skin, which is why clipping and scrubbing the site is a scored skill. Principles of surgery is 12% of the test, and Skills III and VI test site preparation and pack wrapping.',objectives:['Explain asepsis, sterilization and the surgical team’s roles.','Describe preanesthetic care, anesthesia stages and monitoring.','Prepare an operative site following HOSA Skill III.','Wrap a surgical pack following HOSA Skill VI.'],sections:[
['Asepsis and sterilization',[
'**Asepsis** means preventing contamination by microorganisms. **Sterilization** kills all microorganisms, including spores; **disinfection** kills most on surfaces; **antisepsis** reduces microorganisms on living tissue. Instruments are cleaned, packed and sterilized—usually in a **steam autoclave**—with **indicator tape** or strips showing exposure. **Ethylene oxide (gas)** sterilization is used for heat-sensitive items. Sterile items stay sterile until packages become wet, torn or dropped.',
"Surgical instruments are cleaned immediately after use, inspected, packed with an internal indicator and sterilized again before the next surgery.",
'Surgical attire includes caps, masks, sterile gowns and gloves. The surgeon and assistant perform a **surgical scrub**; nonsterile staff (the circulating technician) open packs and manage the room. Only sterile items touch sterile items, and the sterile field is never left unattended.',
match('Asepsis terms',[['Sterilization','Kills all microorganisms including spores'],['Disinfection','Kills most microorganisms on objects'],['Antisepsis','Reduces microorganisms on living tissue'],['Autoclave','Steam sterilizer'],['Indicator tape','Shows sterilization conditions were reached'],['Ethylene oxide','Gas sterilization for heat-sensitive items']])]],
['Preanesthetic care and anesthesia',[
'Before surgery, the patient has a **physical exam**, often **preanesthetic bloodwork**, a signed **consent form** and **fasting** as directed (often several hours for dogs and cats; not for very young or small animals and not water for long). An **IV catheter** allows fluids and emergency drugs. **Premedications** calm the patient and relieve pain; **induction** drugs produce unconsciousness, followed by **intubation** with an **endotracheal tube** placed using a **laryngoscope**, and **maintenance** with inhalant anesthetic (such as isoflurane) in oxygen.',
'Monitoring—by a credentialed technician—includes **heart rate and rhythm**, **respiratory rate**, **mucous membrane color and capillary refill**, **oxygen saturation** (pulse oximeter), **blood pressure**, **end-tidal CO₂**, **temperature** and anesthetic depth (jaw tone, reflexes and eye position). Patients lose heat quickly, so **warming systems** such as forced-air (Bair Hugger) blankets are used. Recovery continues until the animal can swallow, sit up and maintain its temperature; the endotracheal tube is removed when the swallowing reflex returns.',
order('Anesthesia sequence',['Preanesthetic exam, bloodwork and consent','Fast as directed; place an IV catheter','Premedication','Induction','Intubation with endotracheal tube and laryngoscope','Maintenance with inhalant anesthesia and monitoring','Recovery; extubate when the patient swallows'])]],
['Preparing the operative site: Skill III steps 1–9',[
'Skill III has 17 steps in **7 minutes**. **1.** Hand hygiene. **2.** **Verify the patient’s identity.** **3.** Verify the **surgical procedure and site**. **4.** Confirm the animal was **bathed**, the surgical site **clipped and cleaned** and the **bladder expressed**. **5.** Assemble prep materials. **6.** Position the patient on its **back (dorsal recumbency)** and secure the legs with ropes, sandbags, troughs or tape.',
'**7.** Open the sterile packages—**bowl and gauze**—using aseptic technique. **8.** Drop **four gauze sponges** into the sterile bowl and pour **germicide** to cover them, using aseptic technique. **9.** **Don sterile gloves** correctly. Clipping removes hair with a wide margin, and loose hair is vacuumed away from the table before the sterile prep begins.',
order('Before the scrub',['Hand hygiene','Verify patient identity','Verify procedure and site','Confirm bath, clipping and bladder expression','Assemble materials','Position on the back; secure the legs','Open the sterile bowl and gauze','Drop 4 sponges in the bowl and cover with germicide','Don sterile gloves'])]],
['The scrub: Skill III steps 10–17',[
'**10.** Handle sponges with **sterile sponge forceps or the gloved hand** using aseptic technique. **11.** **Begin at the intended incision site** and scrub outward in **concentric circles** to an area at least **two inches larger** than the expected sterile field. **12.** **Discard the sponge** after reaching the edge—never bring it back to the center. **13.** Repeat steps 10–12 **two more times** with fresh sponges (three scrubs total).',
'**14.** **Blot** excess germicide with a sterile gauze sponge. **15.** **Spray 10% povidone-iodine** solution on the site. **16.** Remove gloves and perform hand hygiene. **17.** Use appropriate communication. The **center-to-periphery** pattern moves microorganisms away from the incision; reusing a sponge would drag them back.',
order('Scrub pattern',['Pick up a sponge with forceps or gloved hand','Start at the incision site','Scrub outward in concentric circles','Go at least 2 inches beyond the sterile field','Discard the sponge at the edge','Repeat for a total of three scrubs','Blot excess germicide','Spray 10% povidone-iodine'])]],
['Wrapping a surgical pack: Skill VI',[
'Skill VI has 10 steps in **3 minutes**. **1.** Assemble equipment. **2.** Hand hygiene. **3.** Place a large unfolded wrap **diagonally** in front of you. **4.** Place the instrument tray in the **center** so an imaginary line from one corner of the wrap to the opposite corner is **perpendicular** to two sides of the tray. **5.** Fold the corner **closest to you** over the tray to its far side and **tuck it underneath with a tab out**.',
'**6.** Fold the **right corner** over the pack **two-thirds** of the way to the opposite side. **7.** Fold the **left corner** over **two-thirds** of the way. **8.** Final corner: **a.** fold it down; **b.** tuck the fold **under the previous two folds**; **c.** leave the **tip exposed** for easy unwrapping; **d.** wrap the pack in a **second layer** of cloth or paper and secure the final tab with **autoclave or gas sterilization indicator tape**. **9.** Return equipment. **10.** Hand hygiene. Packs are labeled with contents, date and initials.',
order('Wrap a surgical pack',['Wrap placed diagonally','Tray centered and square to the corners','Near corner over the tray; tuck under with a tab out','Right corner two-thirds across','Left corner two-thirds across','Final corner down and tucked; tip exposed','Second wrap layer','Secure with indicator tape'])]],
['Common procedures and post-operative care',[
'Common surgeries include **ovariohysterectomy (spay)**, **orchiectomy (castration/neuter)**, **dental extractions**, **mass removals**, **cystotomy** (bladder stones), **gastropexy** and fracture repair; in large animals, **castration**, **dehorning** and **cesarean sections**. **Penrose drains** remove fluid from wounds, and **lavage systems** flush contaminated areas.',
'Post-operative care includes **pain management**, monitoring the incision for **redness, swelling, discharge or opening**, preventing licking with an **e-collar**, restricting activity, and removing skin sutures in about **10–14 days**. Clients receive written discharge instructions.',
kase('Surgical prep',{a:{text:'During the scrub, a gauze sponge reaches the edge of the prep area. Should you bring it back to the center? (Scenario invented for practice.)',choices:[['No—discard it and use a fresh sponge from the center','b','Correct. Never move from dirty to clean.',true],['Yes, to save sponges','b','That carries microorganisms back to the incision.',false]]},b:{text:'You notice a small wet spot on the surgical pack’s wrapper. What do you do?',choices:[['Consider it contaminated and replace the pack','c','Correct. Moisture can carry microorganisms through.',true],['Use it anyway','c','Wet packs are not considered sterile.',false]]},c:{text:'You complete three scrubs, blot, spray povidone-iodine and use a fresh sterile pack.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Sterilization kills all microorganisms (autoclave, ethylene oxide); indicator tape shows exposure; wet or torn packs are contaminated.','Anesthesia: exam, bloodwork, consent, fasting, IV catheter, premed, induction, intubation with ET tube and laryngoscope, inhalant maintenance with monitoring and warming, recovery.','Skill III: verify patient and site, bathed and clipped, dorsal recumbency, 4 sponges in germicide, sterile gloves, scrub from incision outward in circles 2 inches beyond, discard at the edge, three times, blot, spray povidone-iodine; Skill VI: diagonal wrap, tab out, right and left two-thirds, final corner tucked with tip exposed, double wrap, indicator tape.'],terms:[['Asepsis','Prevention of contamination by microorganisms.'],['Dorsal recumbency','Lying on the back.'],['Ovariohysterectomy','Surgical removal of the ovaries and uterus (spay).']],mcqs:[q('Where does the operative site scrub begin?','At the intended incision site','At the edge of the clipped area','On the patient’s back','Anywhere','Center to periphery.'),q('How many times is the scrub performed in Skill III?','Three','One','Five','Ten','Initial plus two repeats.'),q('How far beyond the sterile field should the scrub extend?','At least two inches','Half an inch','One foot','It should not extend','Per Skill III.'),q('What is sprayed on the site after blotting?','10% povidone-iodine','Water','Alcohol only','Saline','Per Skill III.'),q('How is the wrap placed in Skill VI?','Diagonally','Square to the tray','Folded in half','Upside down','Corner toward you.'),q('How far are the side corners folded?','Two-thirds of the way across','All the way','Half an inch','Not at all','Per Skill VI.'),q('What secures the final tab?','Autoclave or gas sterilization indicator tape','Staples','String','Nothing','Shows sterilization.'),q('Which tool helps place an endotracheal tube?','Laryngoscope','Hoof tester','Balling gun','Tooth float','Visualizes the larynx.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how to prepare the operative site for a canine spay following HOSA Skill III, and why each step reduces the risk of infection.',rubric:[['Verification and prep',['hand hygiene','identity','procedure','site','bathed','clipped','bladder']],['Positioning and setup',['back','dorsal','secure','sterile bowl','gauze','4 sponges','germicide','sterile gloves']],['Scrub technique',['incision site','concentric circles','outward','2 inches','discard','three']],['Finish and reasoning',['blot','povidone-iodine','skin bacteria','dirty to clean','gloves','hand hygiene']]],model:'I perform hand hygiene, verify the patient’s identity and confirm that the procedure is a spay and the site is the ventral abdomen, so the right animal receives the right surgery. I confirm the dog was bathed, the surgical site clipped and cleaned and the bladder expressed, which removes hair and debris that carry bacteria and keeps the bladder out of the way. I assemble my materials, position the dog on its back and secure its legs. Using aseptic technique, I open the sterile bowl and gauze, drop four gauze sponges into the bowl and cover them with germicide, then put on sterile gloves. With sponge forceps or my gloved hand, I start at the intended incision site and scrub outward in concentric circles to at least two inches beyond the expected sterile field, discarding the sponge at the edge so I never drag microorganisms from the dirtier outer skin back to the incision. I repeat this two more times with fresh sponges for three scrubs total, blot the excess germicide with sterile gauze and spray 10% povidone-iodine on the site to further reduce skin bacteria. Finally, I remove my gloves, perform hand hygiene and communicate clearly with the team.'},sources:[RULES,AAHA_ANES,CDC_STERIL,MERCK]};

export const V10={title:'Identification skills: instruments, equipment and companion animal breeds (Skills I and II)',hook:'A balling gun, a tooth float, a colostrometer and a Barnes dehorner all belong to veterinary medicine—but would you recognize them? And could you tell a Cardigan Welsh Corgi from a Pembroke, or a Korat from a Russian Blue? Skills I and II reward accurate names, correct spelling and clear purposes.',objectives:['Identify veterinary instruments and equipment and state their purposes (Skill I).','Distinguish dog breeds by AKC group and key features (Skill II).','Distinguish cat breeds by key features (Skill II).','Use efficient study strategies for identification skills.'],sections:[
['Small-animal and surgical instruments',[
'Surgical instruments on the HOSA list include **scalpel blades** (cutting skin and tissue), **Mayo scissors** (heavy tissue and sutures), **tenotomy or iris scissors** (fine, delicate cutting), **tissue forceps** (grasping tissue), **needle holders** (holding needles while suturing), **Senn or rake retractors** (holding back skin and tissue), the **Mayo stand** (tray for instruments during surgery), the **surgical pack** (wrapped sterile instruments and drapes), **Penrose drains** (soft tubes that drain fluid from wounds) and the **lavage system** (flushes wounds).',
'Anesthesia and critical-care equipment: **endotracheal tubes and laryngoscope** (secure the airway), the **Ambu bag** (manual resuscitation bag for ventilation), the **pulse oximeter** (oxygen saturation and pulse), the **Bair Hugger warming unit** and **patient warming systems** (prevent hypothermia), the **mobile crash cart** (emergency drugs and equipment) and the **semi-automated external defibrillator** (shocks the heart in cardiac arrest).',
match('Instrument and purpose',[['Needle holder','Holds the needle while suturing'],['Senn retractor','Holds back skin and tissue'],['Tenotomy or iris scissors','Fine, delicate cutting'],['Penrose drain','Drains fluid from a wound'],['Ambu bag','Manual ventilation'],['Bair Hugger','Forced-air warming to prevent hypothermia'],['Laryngoscope','Lights the larynx for intubation'],['Mobile crash cart','Emergency drugs and equipment']])]],
['Large-animal tools, lab and diagnostic equipment',[
'Large-animal tools: the **balling gun** (places pills or boluses in the back of a cow’s or horse’s mouth), **hoof testers** (squeeze the hoof to locate pain), the **tooth float** (files sharp points on horse teeth), the **Barnes dehorner** (removes horn buds or small horns), the **cattle prod** (electric livestock mover—use sparingly), the **mouth speculum** (holds the mouth open for oral exams), the **trocar** (pointed instrument with a cannula, used for example to relieve rumen gas) and the **colostrometer** (estimates colostrum quality).',
'Lab and diagnostic equipment: the **binocular microscope**, **fecal flotation kits**, **reagent strip tests** (urine chemistry), the **Leukopet system** (dilutes blood for manual white cell and platelet counts), **Vacutainer** tubes and holders (blood collection), the **radiograph machine** and **mobile X-ray unit** (imaging), the **autoclave** (steam sterilizer), **splints**, the **sharps container**, **insulin syringes** (marked in units), **butterfly catheters** (short-term IV access or blood draws in small veins), **through-the-needle catheters** (longer IV catheters, often in the jugular) and **urinary catheters** (drain or sample urine).',
match('Large-animal and lab tools',[['Balling gun','Gives boluses or pills to livestock'],['Hoof testers','Locate hoof pain'],['Tooth float','Files sharp points on horse teeth'],['Barnes dehorner','Removes horn buds'],['Colostrometer','Estimates colostrum quality'],['Trocar','Relieves rumen gas'],['Leukopet system','Dilution for manual WBC and platelet counts'],['Vacutainer','Evacuated blood collection tube system']]),
{t:'callout',kind:'exam',title:'Skill I scoring',text:'Skill I has 15 items in 15 minutes: 1 point for the name, 1 for correct spelling and 1 for the purpose (45 points). Practice spelling names such as **Laryngoscope**, **Colostrometer**, **Tenotomy**, **Penrose** and **Vacutainer**.'}]],
['Dog breeds by group I',[
'Skill II gives **15 animals in 8 minutes**—1 point for identification and 1 for spelling. **Sporting Group:** **American Water Spaniel** (medium, curly liver coat), **Brittany** (leggy, orange-and-white, often a short tail), **Cocker Spaniel** (small, long silky ears, domed head), **English Setter** (white coat with speckled “belton” markings), **Golden Retriever** (feathered golden coat), **Irish Setter** (long mahogany-red coat), **Labrador Retriever** (short dense coat, “otter” tail; black, yellow or chocolate) and **Weimaraner** (sleek silver-gray coat, light eyes).',
'**Working Group:** **Alaskan Malamute** (powerful sled dog, plumed tail, brown eyes), **Boxer** (square muzzle, undershot jaw, fawn or brindle), **Bullmastiff** (large, black mask), **Doberman Pinscher** (sleek black-and-rust), **Great Dane** (giant and tall), **Great Pyrenees** (large white guardian with double rear dewclaws), **Mastiff** (massive, fawn or brindle with black mask), **Newfoundland** (giant black water-rescue dog), **Rottweiler** (muscular black-and-tan), **Saint Bernard** (giant red-and-white rescue dog) and **Siberian Husky** (medium sled dog, often blue or bi-colored eyes and a facial mask).',
match('Sporting and Working',[['Weimaraner','Silver-gray coat and light eyes'],['Irish Setter','Mahogany-red coat'],['English Setter','White coat with speckled belton markings'],['Brittany','Orange-and-white, often a short tail'],['Great Pyrenees','Large white guardian with double rear dewclaws'],['Newfoundland','Giant black water-rescue dog'],['Siberian Husky','Medium sled dog, often blue eyes'],['Alaskan Malamute','Powerful sled dog with brown eyes']])]],
['Dog breeds by group II',[
'**Toy Group:** **Chihuahua** (smallest breed, apple head), **Chinese Crested** (hairless with hair on head, feet and tail, or the coated Powderpuff), **Havanese** (silky coat; national dog of Cuba), **Maltese** (long, pure white silky coat), **Pekingese** (flat face, lion-like mane, rolling gait), **Pomeranian** (fluffy, fox-like face, tail over the back), **Pug** (wrinkled flat face, curled tail) and **Shih Tzu** (long flowing coat, often a topknot). **Herding Group:** **Australian Shepherd** (often merle, sometimes bobtailed), **Border Collie** (black-and-white with an intense stare), **Cardigan Welsh Corgi** (short legs with a **long tail**; the Pembroke usually has a docked or natural bobtail), **Collie** (rough or smooth coat), **German Shepherd** (black-and-tan saddle, erect ears) and **Old English Sheepdog** (shaggy gray-and-white coat).',
'**Hound Group:** **Basset Hound** (long ears, short legs, droopy face), **Beagle** (small tricolor scent hound), **Bloodhound** (wrinkled face, top scent tracker), **Dachshund** (long body, short legs), **Greyhound** (fast, slim sighthound) and **Whippet** (smaller, Greyhound-like). **Terrier Group:** **Airedale** (largest terrier, black-and-tan wiry coat), **Miniature Schnauzer** (beard and eyebrows, salt-and-pepper), **Rat Terrier** (smooth, pied coat), **Russell Terrier** (small, mostly white) and **Scottish Terrier** (short legs, wiry black coat, beard). **Non-Sporting:** **Boston Terrier** (black-and-white “tuxedo”), **Bulldog** (wrinkled, pushed-in face, stocky), **Chow Chow** (blue-black tongue, lion-like mane), **Dalmatian** (white with black or liver spots), **Lhasa Apso** (long coat, Tibetan sentinel) and **Standard Poodle** (curly coat, largest poodle size).',
sort('Which AKC group?',['Toy','Herding','Hound','Terrier','Non-Sporting'],[['Pomeranian',0],['Border Collie',1],['Bloodhound',2],['Airedale Terrier',3],['Chow Chow',4],['Cardigan Welsh Corgi',1],['Whippet',2],['Boston Terrier',4]])]],
['Cat breeds',[
'Key features help separate similar cats: **Siamese** (pointed coat, blue eyes, vocal), **Balinese** (longhaired Siamese), **Colorpoint Shorthair** (Siamese type with nontraditional point colors), **Oriental** (Siamese body in many colors, large ears), **Birman** (pointed with white “gloves” on all four paws), **Ragdoll** (large, pointed, blue eyes, relaxes when held), **RagaMuffin** (large, Ragdoll relative in many colors), **Tonkinese** (Siamese–Burmese cross with aqua eyes), **Burmese** and **European Burmese** (compact, glossy coats), **Bombay** (solid black “mini panther” with copper eyes), **Russian Blue** (silver-blue coat, green eyes) versus **Korat** (silver-blue, heart-shaped face, green eyes) versus **Chartreux** (blue-gray, copper-orange eyes) versus **British Shorthair** (round face, plush coat, often blue).',
'Distinctive breeds: **Abyssinian** (ticked ruddy coat) and **Somali** (longhaired Abyssinian), **Singapura** (smallest, ticked sepia coat), **Bengal** (leopard-like spots or rosettes), **Egyptian Mau** (natural spots, green eyes), **Ocicat** (spotted domestic breed), **Maine Coon** (very large, tufted ears, bushy tail), **Norwegian Forest Cat** and **Siberian** (large with thick water-resistant coats), **Persian** (long coat, flat face) and **Exotic** (shorthaired Persian), **Manx** (tailless), **American Bobtail**, **Japanese Bobtail** and **Toybob** (short tails; the Toybob is very small), **American Curl** (ears curl back), **Scottish Fold** (ears fold forward), **Cornish Rex**, **Devon Rex**, **Selkirk Rex** and **LaPerm** (curly or wavy coats), **American Wirehair** (crimped wiry coat), **Sphynx** (hairless), **Lykoi** (partly hairless “werewolf” look), **Havana Brown** (chocolate coat, green eyes), **Khao Manee** (white, often odd-eyed), **Burmilla** (silver tipped coat), **Turkish Angora** (silky, often white) and **Turkish Van** (white with color on the head and tail).',
match('Cat breed clues',[['Birman','Pointed with white gloves on all four paws'],['Scottish Fold','Ears fold forward'],['American Curl','Ears curl back'],['Manx','Tailless'],['Sphynx','Hairless'],['Turkish Van','White with color on head and tail'],['Korat','Silver-blue with a heart-shaped face'],['Chartreux','Blue-gray with copper-orange eyes'],['Bombay','Black with copper eyes'],['Maine Coon','Very large with tufted ears']])]],
['Study strategies',[
'Make **flashcards** with a photo on one side and the name (spelled correctly), group or category and purpose on the other. Study by **contrast pairs**—Cardigan versus Pembroke Corgi, Russian Blue versus Korat versus Chartreux, Greyhound versus Whippet, Siamese versus Balinese versus Colorpoint. Practice writing names from memory and checking spelling, since spelling earns half the points.',
kase('Identification practice',{a:{text:'A photo shows a short-legged herding dog with a long, fox-like tail. (Scenario invented for practice.) Which breed?',choices:[['Cardigan Welsh Corgi','b','Correct. The long tail separates it from the Pembroke.',true],['Dachshund','b','Dachshunds are hounds with longer bodies and no herding build.',false]]},b:{text:'Next: a blue-gray cat with copper-orange eyes. Which breed?',choices:[['Chartreux','c','Correct. Russian Blues and Korats have green eyes.',true],['Russian Blue','c','Russian Blues have green eyes.',false]]},c:{text:'You write each name carefully and check spelling before moving on.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Skill I: 15 items—name, spelling and purpose; know surgical tools (needle holders, Senn retractors, Penrose drains), anesthesia and critical care (laryngoscope, Ambu bag, Bair Hugger, crash cart) and large-animal tools (balling gun, hoof testers, tooth float, Barnes dehorner, colostrometer, trocar).','Skill II: 15 animals in 8 minutes—identification and spelling; learn the dogs by AKC group (Sporting, Working, Toy, Herding, Hound, Terrier, Non-Sporting) and their key features.','Separate look-alike cats by eye color, ears, coat and tail (Russian Blue, Korat, Chartreux; Birman, Ragdoll; Scottish Fold, American Curl; Manx and bobtails; rex breeds).'],terms:[['Balling gun','A tool that places pills or boluses in a large animal’s mouth.'],['Tooth float','A rasp used to file sharp points on horse teeth.'],['AKC group','A category of dog breeds, such as Sporting or Herding.']],mcqs:[q('A tooth float is used to:','File sharp points on horse teeth','Clean dog teeth','Extract cat teeth','Measure colostrum','Equine dentistry.'),q('A colostrometer measures:','Colostrum quality','Blood pressure','Urine specific gravity','Hoof pain','Newborn livestock care.'),q('Which tool locates hoof pain?','Hoof testers','Balling gun','Trocar','Laryngoscope','Squeeze the hoof.'),q('Which AKC group includes the Border Collie?','Herding','Sporting','Working','Terrier','Herding dogs.'),q('Which dog breed is a Toy breed?','Pomeranian','Weimaraner','Boxer','Beagle','Fox-like and fluffy.'),q('Which cat breed has white “gloves” on all four paws?','Birman','Siamese','Persian','Bengal','Pointed with gloves.'),q('A cat with ears that fold forward is a:','Scottish Fold','American Curl','Manx','Sphynx','Folded ears.'),q('Which equipment provides forced-air warming during surgery?','Bair Hugger','Ambu bag','Crimper','Hoof tester','Prevents hypothermia.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how you would prepare for Skills I and II, and give the name, purpose or identifying features of three instruments and four breeds from the HOSA lists.',rubric:[['Study strategy',['flashcards','photo','spelling','contrast','purpose','practice']],['Instruments',['balling gun','tooth float','laryngoscope','needle holder','colostrometer','bair hugger','penrose','purpose']],['Dog breeds',['group','corgi','long tail','weimaraner','silver','great pyrenees','dewclaws']],['Cat breeds',['birman','gloves','chartreux','copper','scottish fold','manx','korat']]],model:'For Skills I and II, I would make flashcards with a photo on one side and the correctly spelled name plus the purpose or identifying features on the other, then practice writing names from memory because spelling earns points, and study look-alike pairs side by side. For instruments, a balling gun places pills or boluses at the back of a cow’s or horse’s mouth; a tooth float is a rasp that files sharp points on horse teeth; and a laryngoscope lights and exposes the larynx so an endotracheal tube can be placed for anesthesia. For dog breeds, the Cardigan Welsh Corgi is a short-legged member of the Herding Group with a long, fox-like tail, which distinguishes it from the usually bobtailed Pembroke; the Weimaraner is a Sporting dog with a sleek silver-gray coat and light eyes; and the Great Pyrenees is a large white Working dog with double dewclaws on its hind legs. For cats, the Birman is a pointed, blue-eyed cat with white “gloves” on all four paws, while the Chartreux is a sturdy blue-gray cat with copper-orange eyes, unlike the green-eyed Russian Blue and Korat.'},sources:[RULES,AKC,CFA,MERCK]};
